<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="methyl indole-3-acetate interconversion" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6303" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: INDOLE_ACETATE_AUXIN (Indole-3-yl)acetate| (IAA) is an abundant form of the potent phytohormone auxin. It affects many aspects of plant growth and development and also participates in responses to biotic and abiotic stimuli |CITS: [15749753] [18541211]|. IAA can be conjugated to a number of small molecules, including sugars and amino acids and it can be modified with additional functional groups, including a methyl group. All of these additions, including methylation, can affect the biological activity and biochemical properties of this important hormone |CITS: [15749753]|. The biological relevance of |FRAME: CPD-10546| (MeIAA) is still under investigation. Some experiments suggest that MeIAA is an inactive form of IAA, although the addition of a methyl group to IAA, which converts it from an acid to a nonpolar compound, should enable it to cross membranes more easily |CITS:[18467465]|. Thus endogenous MeIAA, like methyl salicylate |CITS:[17916738]|, may be a transport form of the hormone that is moved from its site of production to a site of auxin activity and then transformed into active IAA by |FRAME: AT3G10870-MONOMER| |CITS:[18467465]|. Several studies have shown that the exogenous application of MeIAA can cause auxin-like phenotypic changes in plants, and, in some cases, the phenotypes are even stronger than when IAA is applied directly |CITS:[16169896][17926040]|. Therefore, if MeIAA is inactive, this suggests that plants must have adequate levels of MeIAA methylesterase activity to convert exogenously applied MeIAA to IAA. MeIAA is difficult to detect in plants |CITS:[16169896][18467465]|. It accumulates at very low levels in Arabidopsis |CITS:[18367217]|, but it has been observed in a number of plant species (catalogued in &lt;A href=&quot;http://kanaya.naist.jp/knapsack_jsp/information.jsp?sname=C_ID&amp;word=C00000101&quot;&gt;KNApSAcK&lt;/A&gt;). In addition, the phenotypic effects caused by changes the levels of |FRAME: AT5G55250| or |FRAME: AT3G10870| activity in plants argue that this pathway is important for normal plant growth and development |CITS:[16169896][18467465]|. Additional work will be needed to clarify the biological role of MeIAA and to identify the taxonomic distribution of this pathway that can reversibly methylate an important regulator of plant growth, development, and environmental response. Please note: Neither MeIAA nor MeIAA methylesterase activity have been experimentally verified in |FRAME:TAX-3694| or |FRAME:TAX-3330|, even though both have active IAA methyltransferases |CITS: [17499822][19369216]|. However, it seems likely that the reverse reaction occurs. Notably, |FRAME:TAX-3694| and other |FRAME:TAX-3328| species do have predicted proteins with high similarity to the MeIAA methylesterase from Arabidopsis.</Description>
